            <div xml:id="d37">

                <pb facs="0064" n="46" sameAs="frus:frus1946v02/pg46"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Council/4–946: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">urgent   niact</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-09T18:00:00+01:00">April 9, 1946—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 9—1:21 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1691. For the Secretary. Following is translation of French reply dated April 9
                    to your message suggesting a meeting of the Foreign Ministers in Paris. (Dept’s
                    1537, April 4.)<anchor n="68" xml:id="d37-f1"/></p>
                <pb facs="0065" n="47" xml:id="pg47"/>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>Mr. Ambassador: On April 5 Your Excellency was good enough to communicate to
                        me a message from the Secretary of State proposing a meeting in Paris on
                        April 25 of the Council of Foreign Ministers prior to the conference which
                        is to be held the first of May to elaborate the peace treaties.</p>
                    <p>Furthermore, Mr. Byrnes suggested that instructions be sent by each of the
                        Foreign Ministers to their delegates at London charged with the preparation
                        of the draft treaties with a view to accelerating the progress of their
                        work, leaving to the Council of the Four Foreign Ministers the task of
                        finding solutions for questions on which agreement was not reached.</p>
                    <p>I have the honor to inform Your Excellency that the French Govt accepts the
                        above proposition of the Secretary of State and is disposed, for its part,
                        to follow the envisaged procedure. It (the French Govt) holds itself ready
                        furthermore to address an invitation to the govts participating in the
                        Council.</p>
                    <p>Please accept, Mr. Ambassador, the assurances of my highest consideration.
                        Signed: Bidault.</p></quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
